<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tour de Vésone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Tower of Vesunna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tour de Vésone is the surviving round cella of a Gallo-Roman temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built in the 2nd century CE in the Roman town of Vesunna. About 24 metres high, it was dedicated to the town's tutelary goddess Vesunna. The tower lost part of its wall in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and legend explains the gap as the departure of the devil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tour-de-vesone.jpg" alt="Tour de Vésone" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9rigueux_-_Tour_de_V%C3%A9sone_-_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
